"First, we determine the number of ways to choose which two dice out of the six will show a 1 and which two will show a 2. This can be done in $\\binom{6}{2} = 15$ ways.\n\nNext, we determine the number of ways to arrange the two 1s and two 2s among the six dice. This can be done in $2! = 2$ ways.\n\nTherefore, the total number of favorable outcomes is $15 \\times 2 = 30$.\n\nThe total number of possible outcomes when rolling six dice is $6^6 = 46656$.\n\nThus, the probability is $\\frac{30}{46656} = \\boxed{\\frac{5}{1596}}$."
